01. Identity-Based vs Resource-Based Policies:

Another interview topic.

Identity-based policy

Attached to:
User
Group
Role

Example:

Role
 ↓
Allow S3 GetObject
Resource-based policy

Attached to a resource.

Example:

S3 Bucket
 ↓
Bucket Policy

A bucket policy can specify who is allowed to access the bucket.




02. IAM Role in DevOps:
This is where IAM becomes particularly important for your career.

Imagine your pipeline:

Developer
   ↓
GitHub
   ↓
GitHub Actions
   ↓
AWS

The pipeline needs permission to deploy.

Bad:

GitHub
 ↓
AWS access key stored permanently

Better modern approach:

GitHub Actions
       ↓
OIDC
       ↓
Assume IAM Role
       ↓
Temporary credentials
       ↓
AWS

This is an important real-world DevOps security pattern.


03. IAM Role for EKS
Suppose you later work with:

Amazon EKS

Kubernetes workloads may need access to AWS services.

Instead of embedding AWS keys inside pods:

Pod
 ↓
AWS Access Key ❌

use AWS IAM-based workload identity mechanisms.

Conceptually:

Kubernetes Workload
        ↓
IAM Role
        ↓
AWS service

This follows the same principle:

Don't hard-code long-lived credentials into applications.



04. IAM Role for Terraform
Suppose you're using Terraform.

Developer
    ↓
Terraform
    ↓
AWS

Terraform needs permissions to create:

VPC
EC2
ALB
RDS
S3

A common production approach is for Terraform to authenticate using an assumed IAM role rather than embedding long-lived access keys in code.

Terraform
    ↓
Assume Role
    ↓
IAM Role
    ↓
AWS resources
